# The highest GLIBC_* version the shipped Linux binary is allowed to require.
#
# This is a decision, not an observation. glibc is backward compatible and not
# forward compatible, so the floor is set by the machine a release is built on,
# and raising this number quietly drops distributions without anything failing.
# The CI job that builds the packages reads this file and refuses a build that
# exceeds it.
#
# What each floor costs, measured:
#
#   2.43  built on the development machine  - nothing in the world runs it
#   2.39  built on debian:trixie (2.41)     - drops Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12, RHEL 9
#   2.35  built on debian:bookworm (2.36)   - drops RHEL 9 only
#   2.34  would need an older container still, and reaches RHEL 9
#
# Two things hold the floor up, and neither is this project's own code.
# `acosf` and `atan2f` bind to GLIBC_2.43 on any host running glibc 2.43 or
# later, because that release moved their SVID error handling to compat
# symbols. `pidfd_getpid` and `pidfd_spawnp` come from Rust's standard library
# and exist from 2.39; on bookworm they do not exist at all, so no reference is
# created and the floor falls to 2.35.
#
# Do not be misled by `objdump -T` marking the pidfd pair as weak. A symbol's
# weak binding never reaches `.gnu.version_r`, which is what the loader
# consults - `readelf -V` prints "Flags: none" against every GLIBC_* entry - so
# the loader treats the version requirement as mandatory and refuses to start.
GLIBC_2.35
